impl.device_only:
CI or a device has to check:
- Windows runner: compiles only in CI (`release-ui.yml` with `windows_verify_only`, MSVC /W4 /WX). On a device:
  - a custom shape such as browser grab, and a Mac host's open hand, shows as the real cursor with the right hotspot and size;
  - `GetGuiResources(GR_USEROBJECTS)` stays flat across 200 shape changes;
  - a 240 fps video before and after.
- Linux runner: compiles only in CI's linux-app job (`flutter build linux --debug`, `check-linux-bundle.sh`, `linux-desktop-video.sh --software`). On a device, X11 and Wayland:
  - the cursor shows on the toplevel window and switches back to named cursors;
  - Wayland compositor size caps;
  - at scale 2 on X11, GDK truncates the logical hotspot to whole pixels, so an odd physical hotspot can land 1 physical pixel off.
- iOS: CI's ios-app job must confirm the iOS target still builds. DesktopPointer.swift is in the macOS target only and also wrapped in `#if os(macOS)`.
- macOS, by eye with a real mouse over desktop_ui_host:
  - bitmap appearance, scale and hotspot;
  - that FlutterView's `cursorUpdate:` restores our cursor (window re-entry, closing a context menu);
  - that the busy band shows the hourglass rather than an arrow;
  - that leaving the picture restores the arrow.
- macOS CI with Flutter 3.44.9: the macos-app job.
- Painted-pointer latency before and after: needs a 240 fps phone video.

findings.issue:
The new tests fail on the old latch, as claimed, but they do not pin several parts of the per-tick design. With any one of the following mutations, all 14 qos tests still pass:
- the higher-of-two-ends bpf integration reduced to the end value only, or the start value only;
- paced_low checked at the tick's end only;
- the six-unique-frames gate dropped;
- the half-tick fold dropped;
- mean pace taken from the published pace instead of the frames produced.
The Ticks simulator applies every bpf change exactly at a tick boundary, so a cut in the middle of a tick, or an encoder that lags a tick, is never modelled.

findings.evidence:
I spliced the new test module onto 1515eda's qos.rs. Four latch tests fail there (per-second latched after 5 s, mid-window cuts latch, content floor latches, no latch within 12 ticks at pace 30), so the tests do catch the old design. I then applied each mutation to latch_tick on this branch and ran `cargo test -p pb-mapper-desktop --lib qos::tests`. The mutations were:
- expected_bits = self.bpf × n;
- expected_bits = self.span.bpf × n;
- paced_low = low(pace);
- the `span.unique < DYNAMIC_UNIQUE_FRAMES` condition removed;
- the `elapsed < QOS_TICK / 2` guard disabled;
- mean_pace = self.span.pace;
- LATCH_PACE_SHARE set to 0.3 or 0.9.
All leave 10/10 qos::tests passing. Removing the share term fails only the content-floor test. Removing paced_low or widening LATCH_SPAN to 3 s fails only the per-second tests.

findings.fix:
Add small tick-level tests:
- a budget that sizes a tick's frames at the bpf in force before a cut made during that tick, repeated with deep cuts at pace 30, which would latch under end-only integration;
- a tick whose start pace was above 0.75 × nominal, which must not count as paced low;
- a span with fewer than 6 unique frames (mostly duplicates) and a large overshoot, which must not latch;
- a call to tick() under QOS_TICK / 2 after the last one, which must fold into the next bucket;
- a sparse-content per-frame case where frames produced fall well below the published pace, which pins mean pace as frames produced.
